# SYNOPSIS

```usage
netd
```

# DESCRIPTION

`netd` is the network.
It runs the TCP/IP stack `vx-net` over the Ethernet frames of the network driver posted as `/srv/ether0`, such as drv-virtio-net(3), and serves the tree Plan 9's `ip(3)` has, mounted on `/net`: the interface, a directory of conversations for each protocol, the connection server and the name server.
It takes no arguments.

svcd(8) starts it from its manifest, gives it a connector to `/srv/ether0` (`connect=ether0`) and posts it as `/srv/net`, restarting it if it exits.
The console shell's namespace mounts it on `/net`, as the POSIX namespace does, where the C library's sockets use it.

`/net` is served from the start, with or without a driver, since every namespace that mounts it connects when its program starts.
`netd` asks the driver for a session at once, and every second until one is made; once the driver says its MAC address and MTU, `netd` starts DHCP.
When the driver goes, `netd` asks again, and the stack keeps its state meanwhile.

The stack is IPv4 alone: Ethernet, ARP, IP without fragments (a fragment that arrives is dropped, and nothing sent is larger than the MTU), ICMP echo, UDP, TCP, a DHCP client and a DNS stub resolver.
A packet for 127/8, or for the interface's own address, never reaches the wire: it is looped back, so programs on one machine talk over `localhost`.
A packet from 127/8 that arrives on the wire is dropped.
`netd` answers ICMP echo requests for its addresses itself.

The address comes from DHCP (RFC 2131), as the interface's `ctl` file may set instead.
`netd` asks for a mask, a router and a DNS server, renews the lease at the server's T1, or half the lease, and rebinds past T2, or seven eighths; a lease that runs out, or a NAK, starts DHCP again.
A missing mask, or one whose ones are not contiguous, is taken as /24, and a lease under 60 seconds as 60.
Each time the address changes `netd` says so on the console.

Every file is owned by `net`.
The tree is:

```text
/net/ipifc/0/ctl
/net/ipifc/0/status
/net/icmp/clone   /net/icmp/N/...
/net/udp/clone    /net/udp/N/...
/net/tcp/clone    /net/tcp/N/...
/net/cs
/net/dns
```

@node=ipifc title="The interface" keys=ipifc,address,dhcp,static

There is one interface, `0`.

: `/net/ipifc/0/status`
  One line: `dev=` and the driver's post, `ether0`, or `none` with no driver; `addr=` and the address and mask length, or `none`; with an address, `gw=` and the gateway; then `dhcp`, with `lease=` and the seconds granted once there is an address, or `static`.
  Before the driver has said its MAC address, the line ends after `addr=none`.
: `/net/ipifc/0/ctl`
  Takes a message, as below.
  Reading it returns nothing.

A write to `ctl` is one message, its words separated by spaces, a final newline ignored.
Before the driver has first answered, every message fails.

: `add` <addr>`/`<bits> [<gw>]
  Sets a static address: <addr> a dotted IPv4 address, <bits> the mask's length, 1 to 32, and <gw> the gateway, none if left out.
  DHCP stops.
: `dhcp`
  Forgets the address and starts DHCP from the beginning.

@node=conv title="Conversations" keys=conversation,clone,ctl,data,listen,announce,connect,hangup,headers

`/net/icmp`, `/net/udp` and `/net/tcp` each hold a `clone` file and a directory for each of the protocol's conversations, named by its number.
A conversation is an endpoint: one protocol, a local port and, once connected, a remote address.
An ICMP conversation's port is the echo identifier, as in Plan 9.

Opening `clone` makes a conversation, and the fid opened becomes its `ctl`; reading it says the number.
A conversation lasts while any of its files is open; when the last is closed it goes, though a TCP connection finishes closing first.
A fid that outlives its conversation finds nothing there, even if the number has been reused.

Opening `clone` before the driver has first answered fails.

## Files

: `clone`
  Opened, makes a conversation, as above.
: <n>`/ctl`
  Reading returns the conversation's number, <n>.
  Writing takes a message, as below.
: <n>`/data`
  UDP and ICMP: a read returns one datagram, waiting for one, and a write sends one, all of it or none.
  A read too short for the datagram loses the rest.
  An ICMP write is the whole message, type and code first, at least 8 bytes; `netd` fills in the identifier and the checksum.
  ICMP reads return the echo replies sent to the conversation's identifier, and nothing else.
  TCP: the data is a stream.
  A read returns what has arrived, waiting until something has, and 0 at the end of the stream; a write takes what fits in the connection's 64 KiB of buffer, waiting only if nothing does.
  Data written before the connection is made goes once it is.
: <n>`/local`
  The local address and port, as <addr>`!`<port>: the interface's address, or the remote's when that is in 127/8.
: <n>`/remote`
  The remote address and port, `0.0.0.0!0` until connected.
: <n>`/status`
  TCP: the connection's state, as Plan 9 names it: `Closed`, `Listen`, `Syn_sent`, `Syn_received`, `Established`, `Finwait1`, `Finwait2`, `Closing`, `Time_wait`, `Close_wait` or `Last_ack`.
  UDP and ICMP: `Open` once connected, `Announced` once it has a local port, else `Closed`.
: <n>`/listen`
  TCP only, once announced.
  Opening it waits for a connection to come, and the fid opened becomes the new conversation's `ctl`, as `clone`'s does.
  Up to 4 connections wait to be taken; more are refused.

Only `ctl`, `data`, `clone` and `listen` may be opened for writing.

## Control

A write to a conversation's `ctl` is one message, its words separated by spaces, a final newline ignored.

: `connect` <addr>[`!`<port>]
  Connects to <addr>, a dotted IPv4 address; `netd` does not look up names, which `/net/cs` does.
  UDP and TCP need a <port>.
  A conversation with no local port is given a free one, from 49152 to 65535.
  The conversation then sends only there, and takes only what comes from there.
  For TCP the write returns once the connection is made, or fails with why it was not: `connection refused`, `connection timed out` or `i/o on hungup channel` (a reset).
: `announce` <port>
  Binds the local port <port>, which may be written `*!`<port>; `0` picks a free one.
  On TCP it listens: connections that come to the port wait for `listen` to be opened.
  On UDP the conversation receives what comes to the port, from anywhere, broadcasts included.
: `hangup`
  TCP: closes our side; a FIN follows the data written, and reading goes on to the end of the stream.
  UDP and ICMP: does nothing.
: `headers`
  UDP only.
  Each datagram is then read and written after a 52-byte header, as Plan 9's is: the remote address, the local address and the interface's address, 16 bytes each with the IPv4 address mapped into IPv6 (ten zero bytes, two of 0xff, then the four), then the remote port and the local port, two bytes each, high byte first.
  A write's header says where the datagram goes, from the conversation's own port; its local address, interface address and local port are ignored.
  An announced conversation in this mode serves many peers.

@node=cs title="The connection server and DNS" keys=cs,dns,name,resolve,lookup,service

: `/net/cs`
  The connection server.
  Write <net>`!`<host>`!`<service>; then each read returns one line, `/net/`<proto>`/clone `<addr>`!`<port>, one for each address, and 0 after the last.
  <net> is `tcp`, `udp` or `icmp`, and `net` means `tcp`.
  <host> is a dotted address or a name, which is looked up; `*` means any address, for announcing, and is answered as `*`.
  <service> is a port number or one of `echo` (7), `ssh` (22), `domain` (53), `http` (80), `https` (443) and `9fs` (564).
  For `icmp` there is no service, and the query is `icmp!`<host>, answered with no port.
: `/net/dns`
  The name server.
  Write <name> `ip`, or <name> alone; then each read returns one line, <name> `ip` <addr>, one for each address.

Each open of either file is a query of its own, and its answer is kept for that open until another query on it succeeds.
A write is held while the name is looked up, then succeeds or fails with why.
Names are asked of the DNS server DHCP named, over UDP from a random port with a random ID; a reply's lengths are checked, compression pointers are followed only backwards, and only A records for the name asked, or for a name a CNAME chain of at most 8 leads to, are taken.
Answers are kept for their TTL, from 5 seconds to a day, and failures for 10 seconds; at most 4 addresses are kept for a name.
A dotted address is its own answer, asked of no one.
Names are compared in lower case without a final dot.

# EXAMPLES

The manifest that starts it, `boot/svc/netd.ndb`:

```ndb
service=netd program=/boot/bin/netd post=net console restart
connect=ether0
```

What it says as the system starts, under QEMU, and the interface's status:

```text
netd: serving /srv/net
devmgr: started drv-virtio-net
drv-virtio-net: 52:54:00:12:34:56, serving /srv/ether0
netd: 10.0.2.15/24 from dhcp, gateway 10.0.2.2
```

```rc
% cat /net/ipifc/0/status
dev=ether0 addr=10.0.2.15/24 gw=10.0.2.2 dhcp lease=86400s
% ls /net/icmp
clone
```

ping(8) and cs(1) are clients of `/net/icmp` and `/net/cs`, and their pages show what they see.

